{"name":"Creole Cottage","hex":"#e7b89a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"creole","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A language formed from two or more languages which has developed from a pidgin to become a first language.","Alternative letter-case form of Creole (“person born in a colony”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/creole"},{"term":"cottage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small house.","A seasonal home of any size or stature, a recreational home or a home in a remote location."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To stay at a seasonal home, to go cottaging.","To have homosexual sex in a public lavatory; to practice cottaging."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cottage"}],"entries":[{"title":"Creole architecture in the United States","blocks":[{"text":"Creole architecture in the United States is present in buildings in Louisiana and elsewhere in the South, and also in the U.S. associated territories of Puerto Rico and the U.S. Virgin Islands. A variant is Ponce Creole style."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Williamsburg_Natchez_Mississippi.jpg/330px-Williamsburg_Natchez_Mississippi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Creole_architecture_in_the_United_States"},{"title":"Cottage","blocks":[{"text":"A cottage during England's feudal period was the holding by a cottager of a small house with enough garden to feed a family and in return for the cottage, the cottager had to provide some form of service to the manorial lord. However, in time cottage just became the general term for a small house. In modern usage, a cottage is usually a modest, often cosy dwelling, typically in a rural or semi-rural location and not necessarily in England. The cottage orné, often quite large and grand residences built by the nobility, dates back to a movement of \"rustic\" stylised cottages o